label: Proxied County Query
description: >-
  A parcel query that transits app.fetchgis.com, a commercial third party,
  before reaching the county. Iosco and Otsego resolve this way -- see
  apps/etl/src/counties/iosco.ts:15-20 and otsego.ts:15-20 in
  blue-ribbon-properties, which embed the target URL in `proxy.ashx?` and
  send a spoofed Referer.

  The PIN and (for corridor queries) the full buffer geometry are disclosed to
  the proxy operator. Nothing in blue-ribbon records that. Here it is a
  distinct edge type consumed only by a node inside a `zone: third-party-proxy`
  topology, so `weir sys` can answer "what leaves for the proxy" without anyone
  having remembered to write it down.
fields:
  pin:
    classification: linkable
    type: utf8
    label: PIN
    description: The parcel identification number being queried
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 8
      maxLength: 40
  county:
    type: utf8
    label: County
    description: The county whose FeatureServer sits behind the proxy
    nullable: false
    enumValues:
      - Iosco
      - Otsego
  endpoint:
    type: utf8
    label: Endpoint
    description: The county FeatureServer URL, which is embedded as a proxy parameter
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 10
      maxLength: 500
  proxyHost:
    type: utf8
    label: Proxy Host
    description: The third party the query actually goes to
    nullable: false
    enumValues:
      - app.fetchgis.com
  referer:
    type: utf8
    label: Referer
    description: >-
      The Referer header the proxy requires. blue-ribbon spoofs this to
      `https://app.fetchgis.com/?currentMap=iosco`. Carried as data rather
      than buried in the fetch implementation, because it is the closest thing
      this pipeline has to a credential and it should be visible.
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 10
      maxLength: 200
